pub fn title -> String = "Standard Library"

pub fn summary -> String =
  "Choose a standard module for the task: filter a collection, read JSON, or save a file. The directory below lists all 63 modules with their purpose and supported targets."

pub fn chooseTitle -> String = "Choose by task"

pub fn chooseBody -> String =
  "If you use arrays, strings, and functions in JavaScript or TypeScript, start with the operation you need. A module groups related operations: std/text, for example, contains functions for inspecting and transforming strings. Check each API page for argument order, results, and failure conditions."

pub fn collectionsTitle -> String = "Find, transform, or combine elements"

pub fn collectionsBody -> String =
  "Use std/array or std/list for ordered values, std/map for lookup by key, and std/set for unique values. Adding or transforming elements returns a new collection rather than changing the original. A Maybe result distinguishes Just, which carries a value, from Nothing, which means no value was found."

pub fn collectionsLinks -> String = " Compare modules: "

pub fn collectionsCore -> String = " Read collection syntax: "

pub fn collectionsGet -> String = " Look up an element by position: "

pub fn textTitle -> String = "Process text or read JSON"

pub fn textBody -> String =
  "Use std/text for splitting or transforming strings, std/regex for pattern searches, and std/json for parsing JSON and decoding values. For user-perceived characters, check std/text/grapheme. Parsing JSON does not establish that the fields you need have the right types; handle failures when decoding the expected shape too."

pub fn textLinks -> String = " Text modules: "

pub fn jsonLinks -> String = " JSON module: "

pub fn textCore -> String = " Read failure and success results: "

pub fn numbersTitle -> String = "Check numbers or work with bytes"

pub fn numbersBody -> String =
  "Choose between the integer type Int and floating-point type Float rather than treating both as TypeScript number. Some checked operations return Nothing for an out-of-range result; others return a failure reason in Left. Their successful results contain a value in Just or Right, respectively, so check the return type. For large integers, exact decimals, or binary data, check std/big-int, std/decimal, and std/bytes with the Base64 and hexadecimal conversion modules."

pub fn numbersLinks -> String = " Numeric and conversion modules: "

pub fn numbersCore -> String = " Check how value types differ: "

pub fn stateTitle -> String = "Keep changing state or coordinate work"

pub fn stateBody -> String =
  "Use std/ref for state with explicit reads and updates, std/queue or std/deferred to pass values between computations, and std/semaphore to limit concurrent work. These operations run within Effect: a value describing work that does not run merely because you construct it. They do not make ordinary immutable values directly mutable like JavaScript objects."

pub fn stateLinks -> String = " State and concurrency modules: "

pub fn stateCore -> String = " Read how execution starts and reports results: "

pub fn filesTitle -> String = "Use files or run processes"

pub fn filesBody -> String =
  "Validate and combine paths with std/path, then read or write files with std/fs. std/fs supports both process and browser targets but needs a FileSystem service from the execution environment. A service is a value representing a capability that environment supplies. Browser support alone does not grant access to arbitrary operating-system files. Use std/process for arguments and environment variables, std/stdin for standard input, and std/child-process to start another program; these three are process-only."

pub fn filesLinks -> String = " File and process modules: "

pub fn filesCore -> String = " Read how to supply required services: "

pub fn webTitle -> String = "Build a web interface or use the network"

pub fn webBody -> String =
  "std/web/html and std/web/svg build interface content as values on either process or browser targets. The std/web/dom, navigation, storage, and file modules operate on live browser facilities and are browser-only. The std/http client supports both targets and requires an HttpClient service. The HTTP and WebSocket server modules accept connections and are process-only."

pub fn webLinks -> String = " Web and networking modules: "

pub fn webCore -> String = " Read the execution-environment and service boundary: "

pub fn targetsTitle -> String = "Check the target and prerequisites"

pub fn targetsBody -> String =
  "The target labels below describe where a module is available: process for programs run through the CLI, and browser for browser programs. A module supporting both still needs the appropriate services for external input and output. Importing a module does not supply those services."

pub fn prereqBody -> String =
  "If you have not run Seseragi yet, use the first-program instructions. If you only need the call syntax, open the function-application explanation. Effect background helps with state and input/output; you do not need to read every article before looking up a string or array operation."

pub fn runLink -> String = " Run a program: "

pub fn callLink -> String = " Read a call: "
